/** \page examples_guide Examples
\tableofcontents

\section examples_shape The shape of an example

Every example is a complete application built against the engine library. It
subclasses SekaiEngine::Application, pushes a SekaiEngine::Layer::Layer, and
returns the application from SekaiEngine::CreateApplication(), which is the
entry point the engine looks for.

The layer is where a game lives: OnEvent() receives SekaiEngine::Event::Event,
OnUpdate() runs once per frame with the timestep, and OnRender() records the
draws for the frame.

One thing to know before writing an OnRender(): it runs on the update thread,
which does not hold the graphics context, so a draw may only be *recorded* with
SekaiEngine::Render::RenderCommand::Record(). The recorded commands are
replayed on the thread that owns the context, at the end of the frame, in the
order they were recorded.

The examples that load a file use a plain relative path such as
<code>./icon.png</code>. The build copies the referenced assets from
<code>examples/assets</code> next to the executable, so those paths resolve
wherever the executable is run from.

\section examples_basic_window Basic window

The smallest example there is: an application with a layer that does nothing.
Use it as the starting point, and as the check that a fresh toolchain can build
and run a game.

Source: <code>examples/BasicWindow/src/BasicWindow.cpp</code>

\include BasicWindow/src/BasicWindow.cpp

\section examples_input_keys Keyboard input

WASD moves a circle one step per press, read through
SekaiEngine::Input::IsKeyPressed(). Note that IsKeyPressed() is true for one
frame, while IsKeyDown() stays true for as long as the key is held.

Source: <code>examples/InputKeys/src/InputKey.cpp</code>

\include InputKeys/src/InputKey.cpp

\section examples_input_mouse Mouse input

The circle follows the pointer, read through SekaiEngine::Input::GetMouseX()
and SekaiEngine::Input::GetMouseY().

Source: <code>examples/InputMouse/src/InputMouse.cpp</code>

\include InputMouse/src/InputMouse.cpp

\section examples_touch Touch input

Draws a circle under every touch point. GetTotalTouchPoints() reports how many
there are and GetTouchPointPosition() gives the position of each, so one
circle per finger is a loop over the count.

Source: <code>examples/Touch/src/Touch.cpp</code>

\include Touch/src/Touch.cpp

\section examples_draw_cmd Draw commands and text

The drawing example: circles, rectangles, a whole texture, a sub-rectangle of
that texture, and text drawn from a font face. Each of the four labels is
written in English, Vietnamese and Traditional Chinese, and the language is
switched every LANGUAGE_SECONDS, which shows the text engine falling back
across scripts inside a single string.

Two things worth copying from it:

- The order of the Record() calls is the composite order, and the renderer
  rotates a rectangle about its position plus its origin, so Origin is set to
  half the rectangle to spin it about its centre.
- A recorded command keeps the pointer it was handed. String literals are
  fine, but a value formatted at runtime has to live in a member that outlives
  the frame, which is why the frame rate is formatted into m_fpsLabel.

Source: <code>examples/DrawCmd/src/DrawCmd.cpp</code>

\include DrawCmd/src/DrawCmd.cpp

\section examples_sound_music Sound and music

Audio: SekaiEngine::Sound::Sound for a one-shot effect and
SekaiEngine::Sound::MusicStream for a streamed track.

- Space plays the sound.
- P pauses and resumes the music, rate-limited to one toggle per second.
- The bar at the bottom is the playback position, Played() over Length().

Source: <code>examples/SoundMusic/src/SoundMusic.cpp</code>

\include SoundMusic/src/SoundMusic.cpp
*/

/**
 * @page examples_animation Animation
 *
 * @section examples_animation_nodes What an animation is
 *
 * Every animation answers to four calls: Start() to begin (and to report where it
 * begins), Update() to advance by one timestep, IsFinish() to say whether it has
 * run out, and Reverse() to turn around. SekaiEngine::Animation::Node is the
 * interface that says so, and SekaiEngine::Animation::Transition — the easing
 * curve — is one of the things that implements it.
 *
 * A transition moves a value from one end to the other over a duration. Its base
 * class does the bookkeeping — clamping the elapsed time, rewinding, treating a
 * zero duration as a jump to the end — and the curve only answers one question:
 * how far along, as a fraction, is this frame?
 *
 * - SekaiEngine::Animation::Linear, the constant rate, unchanged.
 * - The ten non-linear families of the easings.net catalogue: Quad, Cubic,
 *   Quart, Quint, Sine, Expo, Circ, Back, Elastic and Bounce.
 * - SekaiEngine::Animation::Ease::Mode picks the direction. In spends the whole
 *   shape at the start, Out at the end, InOut splits it across both. It is a
 *   template argument rather than a value passed at construction — a curve is
 *   written SekaiEngine::Animation::Quad&lt;SekaiEngine::Animation::Ease::In&gt;
 *   — so the shape is fixed in the type and no step has to select between the
 *   three. A direction naming none of the three fails the build instead of
 *   quietly running as In.
 * - Back and Elastic overshoot deliberately: Back leaves in the wrong direction
 *   or arrives early, Elastic oscillates before settling. Both still land
 *   exactly on their end value.
 *
 * @section examples_animation_containers Composing animations
 *
 * Three containers are animations themselves, so they nest without anything
 * knowing what is inside them:
 *
 * - SekaiEngine::Animation::Sequence advances exactly one child per call and,
 *   when that one finishes, starts the next within the same advance. Start()
 *   starts only the first child.
 * - SekaiEngine::Animation::Parallel advances every child by the same timestep
 *   and is finished only once every child has.
 * - SekaiEngine::Animation::Repeat runs one child a given number of times,
 *   restarting it at each boundary. SekaiEngine::Animation::Repeat::Forever is
 *   the count that never runs out, and SekaiEngine::Animation::Repeat::Order
 *   picks whether each run starts again from the beginning (Replay) or carries
 *   on from where the last one stopped (PingPong).
 *
 * Reversing a container reverses its children and leaves the structure alone: a
 * reversed sequence still runs its children in the same order, only each one
 * travels the other way.
 *
 * @section examples_animation_manager The animation manager
 *
 * SekaiEngine::Animation::Animator holds the animations a game has handed it and
 * advances them all once per frame, after events have been handled and before
 * the layers update. Play() starts an animation at once — so its handler
 * receives the value it begins at in the same call, and an animation of no
 * duration both receives its end value and is released there — and then queues
 * it. Update() advances what is already running, lets go of everything that has
 * finished, and only then admits what was queued, which is what makes it safe
 * for a handler to Play another animation from inside an advance. Clear() lets
 * go of everything, finished or not.
 *
 * OnTransitionEnd() sets what is told as animations the manager owns finish: the
 * handler runs once for every leaf transition that finishes, whether the manager
 * holds the leaf directly or holds a container that does, in the advance that
 * finishes that leaf and before the container advances past it, with the leaf
 * still alive to look at. No container is reported for itself, and a leaf that
 * runs again inside a repeat is reported again when it finishes again.
 * Discarding is not finishing, so Clear() reports nothing.
 *
 * A game never steps an animation itself: it hands the animation over and the
 * engine advances it from then on.
 *
 * @section examples_animation_example The example
 *
 * The example holds one circle and a label. Its layer builds a
 * SekaiEngine::Animation::Sequence of the seven curves above, wraps it in a
 * SekaiEngine::Animation::Repeat of Forever, and hands that to the application's
 * animation manager from the constructor. Each curve carries a handler of its
 * own that writes the circle's radius. The layer also sets OnTransitionEnd: as
 * each curve ends, the handler refreshes the label to the name of the curve now
 * running — the one after the curve that just ended, wrapping at the end of the
 * cycle — and logs the boundary, so the label is written once per curve and the
 * endless loop comes from the repeat rather than from the end handler. The layer
 * has no update of its own at
 * all: the radius eases through all seven curves and starts again from the
 * first, and nothing in the layer is asked to move anything.
 *
 * Source: <code>examples/Animation/src/Animation.cpp</code>
 *
 * \include Animation/src/Animation.cpp
 */
